<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Yan (206 BC)</h1><p class="st-meta">206 BCE – 202 BCE · capital Ji</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of Zang Tu in the Beijing region, created in 206 BCE, which switched sides to Liu Bang and was ended when its king rebelled against the new Han emperor.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zang-tu">Zang Tu</a> was a general of the revived state of Yan who followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-julu">Julu</a> and into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> heartland. In 206 BCE Xiang Yu made him King of Yan, with his capital at Ji (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>), and moved the previous king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-guang">Han Guang</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>. Zang Tu killed Han <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guang</a> and took Liaodong as well.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Yan was a frontier kingdom facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/donghu">Donghu</a>, distant from the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chu-han-contention">Chu-Han war</a>. In 204 BCE, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-xin">Han Xin</a> conquered Zhao, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zang-tu">Zang Tu</a> submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> on the advice of the strategist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zuoche">Li Zuoche</a>, without a battle.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Soon after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> proclaimed himself emperor in 202 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zang-tu">Zang Tu</a> rebelled. Liu Bang led the campaign himself, captured him and made his childhood friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-wan">Lu Wan</a> King of Yan.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 206 BCE – 202 BCE"><svg viewBox